# Every layer some operation induces into, which is the `categoryValue` population. One row per
# (filing, layer) an induction reaches, and nothing else.
#
# The inductions get an element of their own
#   A second lane set, beside the draws', would give every layer two `lane` elements with nothing
#   but a matching name to say they are one layer, so the key `(filing, layer)` would not survive.
#   BPMN has a second mechanism for this: the category.
#
# A category does not divide anything
#   That is why it fits. `tFlowElement` carries `categoryValueRef` with `maxOccurs="unbounded"`, so
#   a flow element may be in many categories, where `flowNodeRef` puts it in one lane. The draws
#   contain and the inductions cover, and BPMN has one element for each. A second lane set would
#   add lanes and break one lane per layer; this adds none.
#
# What it loses
#   `tCategoryValue` carries a `value` of type `xs:string` and no reference attribute of any kind,
#   so the layer arrives as text beside a `lane` of the same name, and nothing in the document ties
#   the two. It is the weaker cousin of the `callActivity` defect: a call loses the layer outright,
#   a category keeps its name and loses the pointer.
#
# One row per layer, not per induction
#   A categoryValue is one element however many operations refer to it.
#   diagrams/category_members.sqlc is the per-induction population.
-- pm:Operation/pm:Induction projected to the layers it reaches; BPMN 2.0 tCategoryValue.
SELECT DISTINCT i.filing, i.layer
FROM (
    :compose(entries/inductions.sqlc)
) i
